<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.convivy.run-observer</string>

    <!-- Reports what happened to runs that nothing else reports.

         THREE FINDINGS, not one. A run that died without ever finalizing (below),
         a run that died with a working recovery target beside it and had the
         fail-over REFUSED, and a run that finished at a provider nobody chose
         because the fail-over was TAKEN. The epic's rule is that a refusal is
         reported as loudly as an action and neither is satisfied by a field
         written to metadata that nothing reads — and measured over 13,707
         June-August 2026 run directories, 103 refusals and 68 fail-overs had
         exactly one reader in the whole repo, `agentctl status <run-id>`, which
         someone has to think to run against a run id they must already hold.
         48 of the refusals were review agents attached to a pull request.
         (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment: XML forbids one inside a
         comment, `plutil -lint` accepts it anyway, and the repo's plist test
         is the check that catches it.)

         The first sweep announces the whole backlog once and the Library's per-run
         dedup silences it thereafter, which is what the abandonment finding
         already did with 72. A cut-over date was considered and rejected: it is a
         way of not looking, and `started_at` is agent-written anyway, so it would
         not even be a reliable one.

         Reports runs that died without ever finalizing.

         A dispatched run classifies its own death: `_finalize_run` writes
         outcome.json with the exit status, the cost and the harness's terminal
         reason. Which is exactly why this exists — when the process disappears
         (SIGKILL, the machine sleeping, an OOM, the harness dying) there is no
         in-process moment left in which to notice. The run leaves a meta.json, a
         transcript, and nothing else. Nobody is told.

         Measured before it was built: across 10,965 run-* dispatches in
         July-August 2026, twenty ended this way, and ten of the twenty were review
         agents — ten PRs that received substantial review work and no verdict.
         run-af1adcd7 did 444 transcript lines on agent-platform#398 and vanished.
         Both reviewers on vivy#1132 died fourteen seconds apart, so that PR got
         neither.

         WHY NOT READ THE STALE LOG INSTEAD. registry._log_stale already records
         reaped entries, but that log holds 1,414 distinct run ids over the same
         period because healthy runs are reaped there too. Reporting off it alone
         would be about 98.6% noise. The signal is the intersection: the process is
         gone AND no outcome.json was ever written.

         06:30, after the 05:30 reconcile and alongside the morning report, because
         the finding is something the operator reads rather than something that must beat
         another job to the queue. Nothing depends on the ordering.

         Safe to overlap with anything: it reads run directories and writes
         NOTHING to disk at all. It NEVER recovers a run —
         one that vanished mid-flight may have left a worktree, a branch or a
         half-posted comment, and re-running it would duplicate whatever it
         reached. It names what happened and hands it over.

         Findings are announced ONCE per run rather than nightly. They never
         resolve — nothing will ever come back and write the missing outcome.json —
         so an un-deduped alert would repeat forever, which is what trains an
         alerts page to be ignored.

         That suppression is SERVER-SIDE, via the Library's per-run dedup_key, and
         deliberately not a local file. A seen-list kept beside the runs would sit
         inside the tree this audits, which every dispatched agent can write — so
         hiding a run's abandonment forever would be a one-line append, defeating
         this sweep with the exact capability it exists to catch.

         It narrows rather than closes, and bin/run-observer's own comment carries
         the full version: the Library currently accepts any caller-supplied
         dedup_key from the agent role, so an agent holding the same-user bearer
         token can still pre-post the key. Suppressing now costs a credentialed
         call rather than a file edit. -->

    <!-- Deploy-pinned per platform/decisions/0059 rule 1
         (issue_01M19RRWSS9ETR09N9M2BJPA0Q): a timer firing unattended is
         production USE of run-observer, not someone working on it, so this reads
         a COPY installed from `main` into ~/.local/deploy/agent-platform-shared/
         (rules 2, 6, 7) instead of the ~/.local/bin symlink into the checkout —
         that symlink still exists and still reaches the tree, for the person who
         re-invokes run-observer interactively while working on it (rule 5).
         bin/agent-platform-autopull refreshes the deploy area on every
         origin/main advance; bin/install-agent-platform-shared is what actually
         builds it.
         ProgramArguments names the deploy area's own pinned interpreter
         explicitly (rule 4), not the shebang or launchd's PATH. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__HOME__/.local/deploy/agent-platform-shared/venv/bin/python3</string>
        <string>__HOME__/.local/deploy/agent-platform-shared/bin/run-observer</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__HOME__/.local/deploy/agent-platform-shared</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>__HOME__/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>6</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>30</integer>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- STDIO DELIBERATELY OUTSIDE RUNS_ROOT, unlike ~37 sibling plists.

         launchd opens these by plain path with no O_NOFOLLOW. Pointing them at
         ~/Convivy/agent-runs — the tree this job audits, writable by every
         dispatched agent — would let an agent pre-create the log as a symlink to
         any file this user can write (a shell rc file, another LaunchAgent
         plist), and the 06:30 job would then append to it. The write happens
         under a trusted, unattended job rather than in the agent's own reviewed
         transcript: the same laundering that `bin/run-observer` refuses on every
         read it does, arriving through the one path the program does not open
         itself.

         ~/.local/state/agent-platform is where com.convivy.agent-platform-autopull
         already puts its stdio. It NARROWS rather than closes: same OS user, so
         an agent that thinks to plant a symlink there can still do it. What it
         removes is the planting site inside the tree agents already enumerate
         and write to as a matter of course. -->

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__HOME__/.local/state/agent-platform/run-observer.out.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__HOME__/.local/state/agent-platform/run-observer.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
